- Always use python 3.12 or later for all code
- Allways add docstrings and comments
- Dont hardcode any values, use constants instead
- Do not create monolithic module files
- Always add logging 
- Always create tests in a the tests folder. create a mock and a real test in their respective folders
- create temporary files in a temp folder
- Use fastapi when creating APIs
- Create openapi documents and specs when using fastapi
- Follow Python's official documentation and PEPs for best practices in Python development
- For RestAPI, don't build complex APIs if the response can be handled by using base APIs
- ask python binary path once for each session
- Use /Users/jesse.thaloor/Developer/python/vasttams/bin/python for python binary
- Use /Users/jesse.thaloor/Developer/python/vasttams/bin/pip for pip binary
- dont rename fields in the model, use the same name as the field in the database. Ensure these are compliant with the TAMS API.
- when creating a new test or module, check the entire codebase for any existing code that can be reused.
- Allways create CRUD operations for the new module. 
- for vasts3 and vastdbmanager consult ~/Developer/gitlab/vaststore/ for the codebase.
- use TAMS spec compliant ID where ID is required.
- always run tests with feedback and verbose output
- do not run pytest with -q option. For long running tests, use -v option.
- Keep a tab of tests that succeeded and don't them run again until the code behind them changes. Only run tests that failed.
- Dont use UTF icons in logging